  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "What is the coefficient of $(x-1)^{3}$ in the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}$ about $x=1?$",
  "worked_solution": "The coefficient of $(x-1)^{3}$ is $\\frac{1}{3!}{f}^{(3)}(1).$ Let's compute the necessary derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\sqrt[3]{x} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{-2/3} \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =-\\frac{2}{9}{x}^{-5/3} \\\\ {f}^{\u2034}(x) & =\\frac{10}{27}{x}^{-8/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate ${f}^{\u2034}(1),$ which gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2034}(-1)=\\frac{10}{27}\\cdot {1}^{-8/3}=\\frac{10}{27}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3!}{f}^{\u2034}(1)=\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\frac{10}{27}=\\frac{5}{81}.\n$$\n\nSo the coefficient of $(x-1)^{3}$ is $\\frac{5}{81}.$",
